  clock: 3  # ce8d
  nonce: ce8d  # ce8d
  message: "Pass-27 WI-bujot methodological reframing — `is_exported` data IS present in the substrate; the gap is at the Symbol.is_exported field specifically, not at the substrate level.\n\nPass-23 F23.1 / pass-25 F25.7 / WI-bujot title all framed the finding as \"`Symbol.is_exported` is None for 100% of 34,253 nodes\" with the implication that the substrate carries NO is_exported information. Pass-27 cross-checked and found this implication is wrong:\n\n```\nSymbol.is_exported (top-level field on node):\n  None: 34253 (100%)\n\nsupply_chain.is_exported (nested field on node.supply_chain dict):\n  True: 8821 (25.7%)\n  False: 25432 (74.3%)\n```\n\nThe substrate carries is_exported in TWO places via TWO production paths:\n1. `Symbol.is_exported` — ADR-0032 introspection field, set by per-analyzer code, declared in ir.py at the Symbol dataclass top level. **NEVER populated by any analyzer** (Phase 4 sweep claim doesn't hold).\n2. `Symbol.supply_chain.is_exported` — set by the tier classifier's path-based heuristic, using rules like `path matches ^packages/[^/]+/src/` → exported. **Fully populated** on all 34,253 nodes.\n\nSample of an is_exported=True per supply_chain (Symbol.is_exported=None):\n- `TreeSitterAnalyzer` class at `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/.../analyze/base.py:1609-2687` — path matches `packages/[^/]+/src/`, classified exported by heuristic.\n\nSo WI-bujot's underlying concern remains valid (the documented introspection field is unpopulated), but the framing \"the substrate has no is_exported information\" is methodologically wrong. The substrate has TWO is_exported fields:\n- top-level: analyzer-driven introspection (broken at 0%)\n- supply_chain: heuristic (works at 100%)\n\nTwo writers, two production paths, two semantic models, same field name. This is the same family as pass-26 F26.15 (probe-method asymmetry — `.get(key, default)` conflates absence vs None; probe missed because it only checked one location).\n\nImplication for WI-bujot scope:\n- The \"Phase 4 sweep claim doesn't hold for Symbol.is_exported\" framing stays correct.\n- WI-bujot's fix should specifically reconcile the two writers — either remove supply_chain.is_exported (the heuristic) and require analyzer population, OR keep supply_chain.is_exported as the canonical source and have Symbol.is_exported either alias or get dropped. Either way the duplication is a structural issue.\n\nProbe artifact: /tmp/pass27_probe6.py / output at /tmp/pass27_output6.log.\n\nNo status change. Discussion-only update to add the supply_chain.is_exported context that earlier passes missed."  # ce8d
- op: discuss  # c919
  at: '2026-06-02T06:28:26Z'  # c919
  by: agent  # c919
  actor: jgstern_agent  # c919
  clock: 4  # c919
  nonce: c919  # c919
  message: "Pass-28 WI-bujot MATERIAL REFRAMING — the WI's substrate claim is incorrect; the data IS in the substrate at a relocated field path.\n\nWI-bujot states: \"Symbol.is_exported is None for 100% of 34,253 nodes across all 10 mainstream analyzers; Phase 4 sweep claim doesn't hold for this field.\" Pass-27 F27.5 already flagged the suspicion. Pass-28 confirms it conclusively.\n\n**The dataclass field IS populated; the serializer relocates it.**\n\nir.py declares `Symbol.is_exported: bool = False` as a flat top-level field (line 417). But the `Symbol.to_dict()` method (line 457-465) groups it under a nested `supply_chain` object:\n\n```python\n\"supply_chain\": {\n    \"tier\": self.supply_chain_tier,\n    \"tier_name\": _TIER_NAMES.get(self.supply_chain_tier, \"first_party\"),\n    \"reason\": self.supply_chain_reason,\n    \"is_test_file\": self.is_test_file,\n    \"is_example_file\": self.is_example_file,\n    \"is_config_file\": self.is_config_file,\n    \"is_generated_file\": self.is_generated_file,\n    \"is_exported\": self.is_exported,\n},\n```\n\nSubstrate verification:\n```\n'is_exported' as top-level KEY:        present on 0/34253 nodes (KEY ABSENT, not present-and-None)\nsupply_chain.is_exported:              True on 8821 (25.7%), False on 25432 (74.3%)\n```\n\nThe Phase 4 sweep populated `Symbol.is_exported`; the serializer relocated to `supply_chain.is_exported`; the substrate shows 25.7% True. WI-bujot's \"0% populated\" reading came from probing `n.get('is_exported')` on the substrate dict, which returns None for absent keys. Pass-23 F23.1 / pass-25 F25.7 / pass-27 F27.5 / and the WI-bujot title itself all repeat this methodological error — none of them checked `'is_exported' in n` (which would have returned False, surfacing the relocation) and none checked `n.get('supply_chain', {}).get('is_exported')` (which would have returned the actual value).\n\n**Scope of the relocation:** The serializer relocates SEVEN dataclass-declared flat fields to nested supply_chain keys. Pass-28 verified all 7 — see also INV-luhur discussion entry for the spec.md drift on this relocation.\n\n```\ndataclass (ir.py)            | substrate (json)\nsupply_chain_tier            | supply_chain.tier         (rename: strip prefix)\nsupply_chain_reason          | supply_chain.reason       (rename: strip prefix)\nis_test_file                 | supply_chain.is_test_file (relocate, keep name)\nis_example_file              | supply_chain.is_example_file\nis_config_file               | supply_chain.is_config_file\nis_generated_file            | supply_chain.is_generated_file\nis_exported                  | supply_chain.is_exported\n```\n\n**Implication for WI-bujot status:**\n- The Phase-4-sweep-doesn't-hold claim is **false as stated**. Sweep targeted the dataclass field, which is populated, which the serializer relocates.\n- The substrate-side \"no data\" implication is **false**.\n- The legitimate residual concern: the 7-field relocation isn't documented in spec.md line 783 (which mentions only `tier`, `tier_name`, `reason`).\n\nCandidate status flip: `wont_do` with explanation OR re-title to \"Spec.md line 783 documents only 3 supply_chain sub-fields, substrate has 8 — documentation drift\" (rescoped to the actual gap). Defer to human review.\n\n**Same finding-family as F26.15** (probe-method asymmetry between absence vs None). WI-bujot is the SECOND pass-26-era finding to be reframed; F26.15 reframed pass-25 F25.5 and F25.14 with the same diagnosis. Both required a substrate-side absence check + a spec.md re-read to identify the documented serialization rule. Pass-28 sub-pattern note: pass-23 / pass-25 / pass-27 ALL repeated the same probe error on this WI before catching it; the methodological discipline carried forward in F26.15 (`'key' in record`) was sufficient to catch this if applied — but applying it requires also knowing where to LOOK for the populated value when the top-level location is empty (the sibling-field sweep from F27.5 sub-pattern 8 is the missing piece).\n\nProbe artifact: /tmp/pass28_probe1.py + /tmp/pass28_probe2.py / outputs at /tmp/pass28_output[1,2].log."  # c919
